<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ladislaus I of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1040–<a href="/years/1095">1095</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ladislaus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ladislaus I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1077 to 1095. He strengthened royal power and the Church, issued strict laws against theft and defeated Cuman and Pecheneg raiders. After the death of the last Trpimirović king he claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> through his sister Helen, Zvonimir's widow, and occupied much of it around 1091, preparing its union with Hungary. He was canonised in 1192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ladislaus-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Ladislaus I of Hungary" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Medieval masters · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_St._Ladislaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ladislaus was the second son of the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-i-of-hungary">Béla I of Hungary</a> and Richeza (or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois">Adelaide</a>), a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II Lambert</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He and his elder brother Géza were born in Poland, where Béla had settled after being banished, and the family returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> around 1048. After Béla's death in 1063, the brothers acknowledged their cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> as king in a treaty of 20 January 1064, in exchange for their father's duchy, a third of the kingdom. Relations turned tense in the early 1070s, and Ladislaus and Géza defeated Solomon at the Battle of Mogyoród on 14 March 1074. Géza became king, with Ladislaus as his chief adviser.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Géza died on 25 April 1077, and because his sons were minors, his supporters proclaimed Ladislaus king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> resisted with the help of Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, abdicated in 1081 but plotted to regain the crown, and was imprisoned. In 1085 Ladislaus canonised the first Hungarian saints, among them King Stephen I and Duke Emeric, and freed Solomon during the ceremony. His laws, issued after an assembly at Pannonhalma, were severe measures to protect property: thieves were to be hanged, and lesser offenders blinded or sold as slaves.</p><p>When the Croatian nobility divided after the deaths of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-zvonimir">Demetrius Zvonimir</a> and his successor, Ladislaus intervened at the request of his sister Helen, Zvonimir's widow, and invaded in 1091. A local nobleman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-snacic">Petar Snačić</a>, was crowned king and fought on in the Gvozd Mountains, so the conquest was incomplete. Ladislaus founded a diocese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, and the popes, who claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, found him unwilling to accept their claim. He died on 29 July 1095, near the Hungarian-Bohemian border, naming his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos-duke-of-croatia">Álmos</a> as successor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, the nomads of the Pontic steppes did not invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> until the Mongol invasion of 1241. His conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> began the association of the two kingdoms that lasted until 1918. Pope Celestine III canonised him on 27 June 1192, and a pilgrimage to his shrine at Várad became a customary rite for a newly crowned king. Legends present him as a pious knight-king, and the best known tells of his fight with a Cuman warrior who had abducted a Hungarian girl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ladislaus I of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Zvonimir died without heirs, <b>Ladislaus I of Hungary</b> claimed the throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li><li><blockquote>The arrangement made the duke heir presumptive, but it repeatedly led to war: Béla took the throne from Andrew in 1060, and Géza and <b>Ladislaus</b> fought King Solomon in the 1070s.</blockquote><a href="/state/tercia-pars-regni">Tercia pars regni</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1075" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ladislaus I of Hungary"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4450 5804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4450 5804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1075">What was here in 1075? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
